# Task FEATURE-003-US-004-T02 — Test & verify: Poverty & equality categories

> **DaemonCore coding-agent prompt.** Work type: `feature` · Size: `S` · Priority: `P1` · Status: `todo`

## Where this fits
- **Epic EPIC-003 — Deterministic Scoring Engine.** Generate transparent, explainable, deterministic scores across seven policy outcome categories, with a configurable weighting system and per-score rationale. No AI anywhere in the scoring path.
- **Feature FEATURE-003 — Deterministic Scoring Engine — implementation.**
- **Story FEATURE-003-US-004 — Poverty & equality categories.** _As a user, I want Poverty Reduction and Income Equality scores so I can judge distributional effects._

## Objective
Add automated tests and verify acceptance for: Poverty & equality categories.

## What to do
Write **deterministic automated tests** that cover each acceptance criterion of the parent story:
- Assert: Both categories computed deterministically
- Assert: Rationale explains which levers/outcomes drove the score
Use fixed, hand-computed numbers (see `tests/test_fiscal_model.py`). Avoid randomness, network calls and time-dependence.

## Acceptance criteria (Definition of Done)
- [ ] Automated tests cover the story's acceptance criteria
- [ ] Tests are deterministic and pass in CI
- [ ] New/updated tests pass and cover the above
- [ ] No regression in the existing `pytest` suite

## Dependencies
Complete these first: `FEATURE-003-US-004-T01`.

## Project conventions
- Target Python 3.13. Use British spelling in all user-facing text.
- Keep the pure model layer (`model/`) free of Streamlit; `app.py` is UI/render only.
- Pin any new runtime deps in `requirements.txt`; test-only deps in `requirements-dev.txt`.
- Write deterministic `pytest` tests; follow the style of `tests/test_fiscal_model.py`.
- Match the surrounding code style. Prefer small, coherent diffs over broad refactors.
- Update `README.md` / `docs/` whenever behaviour changes.

## Guardrails (EPIC-003)
- Scoring MUST be deterministic — NO AI and no randomness in the scoring path.
- Every score must carry a plain-English rationale.
- Weightings must be externally configurable and documented.
- Normalise scores to the documented common scale (e.g. 0–100).

## Verification
- `python3 -m py_compile <changed .py files>`
- `python3 -m pytest -q` — all tests pass (add/extend tests for this change).

